```json
{"conjecture": "FP_x^2 = FV · FP_y. Equivalently, P_y lies on the parabola with focus F and vertex V, whose directrix is e, and P_x is the foot of the perpendicular from F to the tangent m at P_y.", "reason": "F is the antipode of G, so j (through F, parallel to the tangent at G) is the tangent to c at F. Take F as origin, with the u-axis along j and the v-axis perpendicular to it. Write V=(a,0) and D=(d,0). Using the polar of C, one finds d=2a: the coordinates give FV=2.3094 and FD=4.6188. So h1 is u=a and e is u=2a. Let P_x=(a,t). Then K=(2a,2t), and the line through K parallel to j is v=2t. The line m is a(u−a)+t(v−t)=0, which gives P_y=(a−t²/a, 2t). Setting w=2t gives u=a−w²/(4a). This is a parabola with focus F, vertex V and directrix e, and m is its tangent at P_y. From the focus-directrix property, FP_y = 2a−u = a+t²/a. Also FP_x² = a²+t² = a·FP_y = FV·FP_y. Numerically, FP_x² ≈ 26.200 and FV·FP_y ≈ 2.3094·11.345 ≈ 26.200.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "For every P_x on h1, P_y lies on the fixed parabola with focus F and vertex V. Its axis is the line j = FV and its directrix is e, the line perpendicular to j at D = 2V − F. Equivalently, P_yF = dist(P_y, e) = P_yK. Also, m is the tangent to this parabola at P_y, and P_x is the foot of the perpendicular from F to that tangent.", "reason": "1) GF is a diameter of c, so f ⟂ GF. Since j ∥ f and j passes through F, j is the tangent to c at F. 2) The angle GEF is inscribed in a semicircle, so ∠FED = 90°. The tangent lengths from V are equal, so VF = VE. That makes V the circumcenter of right triangle FED, so V is the midpoint of FD, and FD = 2·FV. 3) h1 and e are both perpendicular to j. h1 passes through V and e passes through D, so h1 lies halfway between F and e. That makes h1 the vertex tangent of the parabola with focus F and directrix e, and the vertex is V. 4) P_x lies on h1, so the ray from F through P_x meets e at K = 2P_x − F, which is the reflection of F in P_x. The line m is perpendicular to FK at its midpoint P_x, so m is the perpendicular bisector of FK. Every point of m is therefore equidistant from F and K. 5) P_y is on m and on the line through K parallel to j, which is perpendicular to e. So P_yK is the distance from P_y to e, and P_yF = P_yK = dist(P_y, e). Hence P_y lies on the parabola, and m is its tangent at P_y (the reflection property). Numerical check: P_yF ≈ 11.345 ≈ P_yK, FV ≈ 2.309, and F to e ≈ 4.619 = 2·FV.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "P_y always lies on the fixed parabola with focus F and vertex V. Its axis is j, its vertex tangent is h1 and its directrix is e. Equivalently, P_yF = dist(P_y, e). The line m = P_xP_y is the tangent to this parabola at P_y.", "reason": "GF is a diameter, so j (parallel to f through F) is the tangent at F. V is where the tangents at F and E meet, so VF = VE. Because GF is a diameter, angle GEF = 90°, so FE ⊥ ED. In right triangle FED, the point V on the hypotenuse FD with VE = VF must be its midpoint, so FD = 2·FV. This makes e (perpendicular to j at D) the reflection of h1 (perpendicular to j at V) in the sense of the directrix/vertex-tangent pair for focus F. P_x lies on h1, the midline between F and e, so the ray FP_x meets e at K = 2P_x − F. Numerically (2·P_x − F) = (3.9585, 5.6606) = K. So m, the perpendicular bisector of FK, satisfies P_yF = P_yK for every point on it. P_yK is parallel to j and therefore perpendicular to e, so P_yK = dist(P_y, e). Hence P_yF = dist(P_y, e) for every P_x on h1. This is the parabola with focus F and vertex V, and m is its tangent at P_y. Numerical check: P_yF ≈ 11.345 and dist(P_y, e) ≈ 11.345.", "closes_goal": true}
```

```json
{"conjecture": "As P_x moves on h1, P_y lies on a fixed parabola. This parabola has focus F and vertex V. Its axis is j, its directrix is e and its vertex tangent is h1. Equivalently, P_yF = dist(P_y, e) = P_yK, and line P_xP_y (= m) is the tangent to that parabola at P_y.", "reason": "1) Line j passes through F parallel to the tangent f at G, and F is the antipode of G. So j is the tangent to c at F. 2) V is where the tangents at F and E meet. So V is the pole of chord FE, and VF = VE. 3) In the right triangle F E D, the angle at E is 90° because GF is a diameter. V lies on the hypotenuse FD and VF = VE, so V is the midpoint of FD. The coordinates confirm this: 2V − F ≈ (5.072, −3.407), and this point lies on GE. 4) Line e is perpendicular to j at D, so e is the reflection of the line through F perpendicular to j across h1. The line h1 is perpendicular to j at V, halfway between F and e. 5) This is the standard parabola construction with focus F and directrix e. Since h1 is midway between F and e, the foot P_x of the perpendicular from F to line m lies on h1. Ray FP_x therefore meets e at K = 2P_x − F, so P_x is the midpoint of FK (numerically (F+K)/2 = (2.223, 0.845) = P_x). 6) Line m is the perpendicular bisector of FK. The line through K parallel to j is perpendicular to e, so K is the foot of P_y on e. Because P_y lies on the perpendicular bisector of FK, P_yF = P_yK = dist(P_y, e). So P_y is on the parabola and m is its tangent there. Numerically P_yF² ≈ 128.708 and P_yK² ≈ 128.709.", "closes_goal": true}
```
